This paper drops a major advance in AI-powered molecular discovery: Instead of testing one molecule at a time, the LIDS method designs mixtures—"smart soups"—that are tested as a whole, then decoded to pinpoint the best candidate.

On simulated DNA and protein tasks, LIDS found the optimal molecule in just 2–3 rounds, versus 27–100+ for leading Bayesian methods. That’s a 10–100x reduction in experiments—moving from linear to logarithmic search complexity.

LIDS is robust to noise and even to 40% corrupted synthesis parameters. The math proves this approach can crack ultra-sparse problems previously thought intractable, opening the door to fully autonomous, self-driving labs.

